\section*{The first lattice correction to quantum Bloch magnetization}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. For the nearest-neighbor model, magnetization deficit is claimed equal to ideal-magnon density up to $o(\beta^{-5/2})$, including the stated $\zeta(5/2)$ lattice correction.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Capacity comparison controls pinned return cycles on a period. Sparse-obstacle propagation and an ideal-potential profile identify comparison sources whose cancellation improves the error beyond the leading Bloch term, allowing the next dispersion coefficient to survive.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
For the nearest-neighbor model, magnetization deficit is claimed equal to ideal-magnon density up to $o(\beta^{-5/2})$, including the stated $\zeta(5/2)$ lattice correction. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 271, the selected theorem constructs low-temperature zero-field magnetized KMS states for all dimensions at least three and all positive spins. The Bloch expansion, lattice correction, and spherical moment law are separate unselected companions. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

